{# One card per lot: the auction sheet on the left, everything read off it on
   the right. Self-contained by construction — the only <img> is a data URI and
   the only external URLs are hyperlinks back to banzai24, which the page never
   needs to load.

   Requirement verdicts print **against the value they judge** rather than in a
   block of their own — the same convention the cross-checks follow, and for the
   same reason: a verdict qualifies a number best when it is printed on it.

   Imported by `report.html.j2` and by the dashboard's search pages. Everything
   it needs arrives as an argument, `jpy_per_eur` included: a macro imported
   without context cannot see the render globals, and a card that silently
   dropped its euro line on one page and not another is exactly the kind of bug
   nobody reports. The Jinja filters it uses — `yen`, `km`, `check`, `verdict` —
   come from the environment, so any page rendering a card must register them;
   see `banzai24.report._environment`. #}

{# The diagram's handwriting sits with its codes, not down in the warnings
   list: ズレ is written across the front bumper and it is that bumper it
   describes. There is no severity digit to lead with, so the Japanese does and
   the gloss follows it — and a word nobody has glossed yet still names its
   panel, which is what sends you to the scan. #}
{% macro diagram_notes(notes) %}
{% if notes %}
<div class="marks">
  {% for note in notes %}
  <span class="mark written" title="written on the diagram, not a legend code">
    <b class="ja">{{ note.ja }}</b>{% if note.en %} {{ note.en }}{% endif %}
    {%- if note.panel %} · {{ note.panel }}{% endif %}
  </span>
  {% endfor %}
</div>
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro card(view, jpy_per_eur=None) %}
{% set lot = view.lot %}
{% set top = view.flags[0].key if view.flags else '' %}
{# The zoom checkbox is keyed on the lot number rather than a loop counter:
   cards are emitted group by group, so a per-loop index would collide across
   headings and one click would zoom a different card. #}
{% set zoom_id = 'zoom-' ~ lot.lot_number %}
<article class="lot {{ 'flagged' if view.flags }} {{ top }}">

  <div class="head">
    <span class="lotno">Lot {{ lot.lot_short }}</span>
    <span class="where">
      {{ lot.auction_name }} · {{ lot.trade_date }}{% if lot.trade_time %} {{ lot.trade_time }}{% endif %}
    </span>
    <span class="badges">
      {% for flag in view.flags %}
      <span class="badge {{ flag.key }}">{{ flag.label }}</span>
      {% endfor %}
    </span>
    <span class="status">{{ lot.status_code or '—' }}</span>
  </div>

  {% if view.verdict_line %}
  <p class="verdict {{ view.group }}">{{ view.verdict_line }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="body">
    <div class="left">
      {# The checkbox precedes .sheet so `:checked +` can reach it; it is visually
         hidden but still keyboard-reachable. #}
      <input type="checkbox" class="zoom sheet-zoom" id="{{ zoom_id }}"
             aria-label="Enlarge the auction sheet for lot {{ lot.lot_short }}">
      <label class="sheet" for="{{ zoom_id }}">
        {% if view.sheet_uri %}
        <img src="{{ view.sheet_uri }}" alt="Auction sheet for lot {{ lot.lot_short }}"
             width="800" height="800">
        {% else %}
        <span class="nosheet">
          {% if lot.sheet_status == 'no_sheet' %}
          banzai24 published no sheet for this lot
          {% else %}
          sheet not downloaded ({{ lot.sheet_status }})
          {% endif %}
        </span>
        {% endif %}
      </label>

      {# The car itself, under the sheet that describes it — as many shots as
         fetch brought down, which is however many fit this one row. There is no
         "and N more" line because the number is not known here and would cost a
         column to store; the lot page in Links is the rest of the gallery. #}
      {% if view.photo_uris %}
      <div class="photos">
        {% for photo_uri in view.photo_uris %}
        <input type="checkbox" class="zoom photo-zoom" id="{{ zoom_id }}-photo-{{ loop.index }}"
               aria-label="View photo {{ loop.index }} of lot {{ lot.lot_short }} full size">
        <label class="shot" for="{{ zoom_id }}-photo-{{ loop.index }}">
          <img src="{{ photo_uri }}" loading="lazy"
               alt="Photo {{ loop.index }} of lot {{ lot.lot_short }}">
        </label>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="fields">
      <h2>
        {{ view.title }}
        {% if view.registration %}<span class="year">· {{ view.registration }}</span>{% endif %}
      </h2>

      <dl>
        {# The trim — *which* of a model's cars this is, as against the Grade
           row below, which is what condition it is in. Two boxes on the sheet,
           both called a grade in English, and on a Harrier the first is worth
           about ¥1.5M.

           Only shown once a sheet has been read: the <h2> above already carries
           the API's own trim line, and a second row repeating it in an unread
           state would push the scan further down for nothing.

           `dd.ja` is pre-wrap, so the whitespace here is controlled — the
           template's own indentation would otherwise print as a blank first
           line and a leading indent. #}
        {% if view.trim %}
        <dt>Trim グレード</dt>
        <dd class="ja">
          {%- if view.trim.unasked -%}
          <span class="unset">this sheet was read before the trim was asked for</span>
          {%- elif view.trim.blank -%}
          <span class="unset">the グレード box is blank</span>
          {%- else -%}
          {{ view.trim.printed }}
          {%- if view.trim.en %} <span class="note">{{ view.trim.en }}</span>
          {%- else %} <span class="note bad">(no entry in cars/inputs/trims.toml)</span>
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if view.trim.note %}<span class="note translation">{{ view.trim.note }}</span>{% endif -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {# The listing's own trim line, against the `[api]` grade rules the
           search splits one car on. Directly under the sheet's box, because the
           whole reason to print a line the <h2> already carries is to have the
           two answers a centimetre apart: the file is re-read every time this
           page is built, so a grade banned since the fetch is a lot sitting here
           that nothing else would mention.

           Shown whenever the search names any grade — like Drivetrain, and for
           the same reason: an empty line is the reason a card wears a badge, and
           hiding the row would hide the reason. #}
        {% if view.check('trim') %}
        <dt>Trim (listing)</dt>
        <dd>
          <span class="{{ view.check('trim') | verdict }}">
            {%- if lot.modification %}{{ lot.modification }}
            {%- else %}<span class="unset">not stated</span>{% endif -%}
          </span>
          <span class="note{{ ' bad' if view.check('trim').failed }}">({{ view.trim_rule }})</span>
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        <dt>Grade</dt>
        <dd>
          <span class="{{ view.check('grade') | verdict or (view.checks.grade | check if view.checks else '') }}">
            {{ (view.extraction.sheet_grade if view.extraction else None) or lot.grade_origin or '—' }}
          </span>
          {% if view.extraction %}
            {% if view.extraction.exterior_grade %}· exterior {{ view.extraction.exterior_grade }}{% endif %}
            {% if view.extraction.interior_grade %}· interior {{ view.extraction.interior_grade }}{% endif %}
          {% endif %}
          {% if view.check('grade') and view.check('grade').detail %}
          <span class="note bad">({{ view.check('grade').detail }})</span>
          {% elif view.grade_note %}<span class="note bad">({{ view.grade_note }})</span>{% endif %}
        </dd>

        <dt>Mileage</dt>
        <dd>
          {% if view.extraction and view.extraction.sheet_mileage_km is not none %}
          <span class="{{ view.check('mileage') | verdict or view.checks.mileage | check }}">{{ view.extraction.sheet_mileage_km | km }}</span>
          {% if view.mileage_note %}<span class="note">({{ view.mileage_note }})</span>{% endif %}
          {% else %}
          {{ lot.mileage_km | km or '—' }} <span class="note">(API, rounded)</span>
          {% endif %}
        </dd>

        {% if view.extraction %}
        <dt>Chassis</dt>
        <dd>
          <code class="{{ view.checks.chassis | check }}">{{ view.extraction.chassis_full or '—' }}</code>
          {% if view.chassis_note %}<span class="note">({{ view.chassis_note }})</span>{% endif %}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {# Drivetrain shows whenever the search asks for one, extraction or not:
           its "?" is the reason a card sits in *unconfirmed*, and hiding the row
           would hide the reason. #}
        {% if view.check('drivetrain') %}
        <dt>Drivetrain</dt>
        <dd>
          <span class="{{ view.check('drivetrain') | verdict }}">
            {{ (view.extraction.drivetrain if view.extraction else None) or 'not on the sheet' }}
          </span>
          {% if view.check('drivetrain').detail and view.check('drivetrain').failed %}
          <span class="note bad">(wanted {{ view.requirements.drivetrain }})</span>
          {% endif %}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {# The damage row is the assertion, not the inventory: when the search
           bans codes, the label says which and the verdict marker answers it.
           The marks themselves are still listed, with the offending one picked
           out — you still have to look at the scan, and the code tells you where. #}
        {% if view.check('damage') %}
        <dt>Damage</dt>
        <dd>
          <span class="{{ view.check('damage') | verdict }}">no {{ view.banned_codes | join('/') }} marks</span>
          {% if view.damage_marks %}
          <div class="marks">
            {% for mark in view.damage_marks %}
            <span class="mark {{ 'banned' if mark.banned }}"
                  title="{{ mark.meaning or 'not in the printed legend' }}">
              <b>{{ mark.code }}</b> {{ mark.panel }}
            </span>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
          {% elif view.extraction and not view.diagram_notes %}
          <span class="note">(diagram clean)</span>
          {% endif %}
          {{ diagram_notes(view.diagram_notes) }}
        </dd>
        {% elif view.damage_marks or view.diagram_notes %}
        <dt>Damage</dt>
        <dd>
          {% if view.damage_marks %}
          <div class="marks">
            {% for mark in view.damage_marks %}
            <span class="mark" title="{{ mark.meaning or 'not in the printed legend' }}">
              <b>{{ mark.code }}</b> {{ mark.panel }}
            </span>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
          {% endif %}
          {{ diagram_notes(view.diagram_notes) }}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {% if view.extraction %}
        {# Item by item rather than as one block of Japanese with one block of
           English under it: the box is a list of separate things wrong with the
           car, and the buyer prices them one at a time. `warnings_note` is the
           old whole-box translation, kept only for a box the glossary cannot
           read a single term of. #}
        {% if view.warnings %}
        <dt>Warnings 注意事項欄</dt>
        <dd>
          <ul class="terms">
            {% for item in view.warnings %}
            <li><span class="ja warn">{{ item.ja }}</span>{% if item.en %}<span class="gloss">{{ item.en }}</span>{% endif %}</li>
            {% endfor %}
          </ul>
          {% if view.warnings_note %}<span class="note translation">{{ view.warnings_note }}</span>{% endif %}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {% if view.extraction.inspector_notes_ja %}
        <dt>Inspector's notes 検査員記入欄</dt>
        <dd class="ja">{{ view.extraction.inspector_notes_ja }}{% if view.extraction.inspector_notes_en %}<span class="note translation">{{ view.extraction.inspector_notes_en }}</span>{% endif %}</dd>
        {% endif %}

        {# Always rendered once a sheet is read, blank box included: it is the
           input the bid falls back to `private` on, and the card has to show
           what that assumption was made from. #}
        {% if view.history_note %}
        <dt>Vehicle history 車歴</dt>
        {# Whitespace-controlled: `dd.ja` is pre-wrap, so the template's own
           indentation would print as a blank first line and a leading indent. #}
        <dd class="ja {{ 'warn' if view.history_note.rental }}">
          {%- if view.history_note.unset -%}
          <span class="unset">unset — the sheet says neither</span>
          {%- else -%}
          {{ view.history_note.ja }}{% if view.history_note.en %} <span class="note">{{ view.history_note.en }}</span>{% endif %}
          {%- endif -%}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {% if view.equipment %}
        <dt>Equipment</dt>
        <dd>
          <details>
            <summary>{{ view.equipment | length }} items</summary>
            <ul class="terms">
              {% for item in view.equipment %}
              <li><span class="ja">{{ item.ja }}</span>{% if item.en %}<span class="gloss">{{ item.en }}</span>{% endif %}</li>
              {% endfor %}
            </ul>
          </details>
        </dd>
        {% endif %}
        {% endif %}{# view.extraction #}

        <dt>Price</dt>
        <dd>
          start {{ lot.start_price_jpy | yen or '—' }}
          {% if jpy_per_eur and lot.start_price_jpy %}
          <span class="note">(≈ €{{ '{:,.0f}'.format(lot.start_price_jpy / jpy_per_eur) }}
            at {{ jpy_per_eur }} ¥/€)</span>
          {% endif %}
          {% if lot.end_price_jpy %}· sold {{ lot.end_price_jpy | yen }}{% endif %}
        </dd>

        {# max_bid − extra_costs = bid_reduced, or the reason there is no such
           number. Printed on every card in every group: a car that fails a
           requirement is still one whose price you may want to know, and the
           arithmetic does not depend on the verdict. Omitted only when the
           tables themselves are missing — that reason is report-wide and prints
           once in the header, rather than sixty times down the page. #}
        {% if view.quote %}
        <dt>Bid</dt>
        <dd class="money">
          {% for line in view.quote.lines() %}
          {% if loop.last %}
          <span class="{{ 'result' if view.quote.bid_reduced is not none else 'why' }}">{{ line }}</span>
          {% else %}
          <span>{{ line }}</span>
          {% endif %}
          {% endfor %}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        {# What that max bid costs on Cyprus plates. Priced from `max_bid` rather
           than `bid_reduced`: the area price is paid in Japan for the car and
           belongs in the customs value. The rates are the ones stamped into the
           run the morning it was fetched, so this number never moves under a
           re-render.

           A string here is a reason the car could not be priced, and prints
           where the money would go. Two reach this page: no model spec at all,
           and a spec carrying no CO₂ figure. The second is not a technicality —
           road tax comes off the Cyprus CO₂ scale and is worth €45 to €1,500 a
           year, so a car with no figure cannot be landed within a hundred euro
           and gets the sentence instead of a total. Both are fixed in
           `cars/inputs/model_specs.csv`, and the reason names it.

           Only the landed cost prints. `Margin.lines()` also carries the resale
           estimate and the gap to it, and those were crowding out the half of
           the card this page is actually for — reading the condition off the
           sheet. The resale side is a curve fitted to asking prices with a
           haircut on top; it belongs in a pricing report, not next to the
           damage diagram. #}
        {% if view.margin %}
        <dt>Landed</dt>
        <dd class="money">
          {% if view.margin is string %}
          <span class="why">{{ view.margin }}</span>
          {% else %}
          <span class="result">{{ view.margin.lines()[0] }}</span>
          {% endif %}
        </dd>
        {% endif %}

        <dt>Links</dt>
        <dd class="note">
          {% if view.lot_url %}<a href="{{ view.lot_url }}">banzai24 lot page</a> · {% endif %}
          <code>{{ lot.lot_number }}</code>
          {% if view.extraction %}· read by {{ view.extraction.model_id }}
          {% if view.extraction.confidence is not none %}at confidence {{ '%.2f' % view.extraction.confidence }}{% endif %}
          {% endif %}
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
{% endmacro %}
